WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:10.399
A Mac has no Cast button. Chrome casts a browser tab, but it won't play an MP3 from your Mac on the TV with proper controls. This native player does.

00:11.149 --> 00:18.622
It finds every Chromecast and Google TV on your network by name. Open an MP3, pick a TV, and press play.

00:18.822 --> 00:29.537
Pause, seek, volume and speed all work, and the controls show what the TV reports. With ffmpeg installed, the TV shows a moving spectrum of the music.

00:30.287 --> 00:33.126
A Chromecast never receives your file directly.

00:33.326 --> 00:45.418
The player finds TVs, opens a TLS session, and launches the default media receiver. Then it serves the file over HTTP from your Mac, and tells the TV where to fetch it.

00:46.168 --> 00:52.654
Discovery uses the dns-sd tool that macOS already ships. Nothing extra to install.

00:53.404 --> 01:02.708
The whole interface is one data file of state machines and skins. The only code is this adapter, which answers each request with the Chromecast package.

01:02.908 --> 01:07.328
An unknown request becomes an error on screen, never a silent no-op.

01:08.078 --> 01:17.157
Every byte goes through one algebraic effect. In the tests, a handler plays the TV, so four whole journeys run with no window and no network.

01:17.357 --> 01:18.421
All four pass.

01:19.171 --> 01:27.144
What to know first. It plays MP3 files only, it runs on macOS, and it does not verify the TV's certificate.

01:27.894 --> 01:34.702
Build Osprey, then open the player. Osprey is actively evolving. Try it on your projects, and tell us what breaks.
